From c3a0d73ce3fc803285e6579da6bcf5e1c1a95800 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: orlfman <25542856+orlfman@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 13:16:01 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 16/21] linux: extend Intel integrated graphics support

The Intel sampler only accepted the Arrow Lake iGPU (8086:7d67) on
i915. Accept the 109 integrated graphics IDs that Linux 7.3 lists for
Tiger Lake, Rocket Lake, Alder Lake, Raptor Lake, Meteor Lake, Arrow
Lake, Lunar Lake, Panther Lake, Wildcat Lake and Nova Lake, bound to
either i915 or xe. Discrete cards (DG1, Arc) and older integrated
families stay "not found". With more than one candidate the lowest
PCI address wins.

On xe, utilization is the ratio of drm-cycles-<class> to
drm-total-cycles-<class> deltas, with each busy value kept paired
with its GPU timestamp: a stalled timestamp keeps the last usable
pair, and a timestamp that goes backwards starts a new interval.
Allocated memory is drm-total-system plus drm-total-gtt. The
requested clock comes from freq0 of the GT that has render or compute
engines.

The engine inventory (card/engine on i915, tile0/gtN/engines on xe)
tells which graphics classes exist, so a GPU with only render or only
compute engines reports utilization from that class, while a missing
counter for a class that exists stays N/A. Without a gt/gt0 directory,
i915's card-level gt_cur_freq_mhz and gt_max_freq_mhz are used.

A descriptor on the selected GPU's node whose fdinfo lacks the driver,
a valid PCI address or a client ID marks the scan incomplete instead
of being skipped as a foreign client, which showed false zeros.
O_PATH references with no drm- keys are not clients and are ignored.

Co-Authored-By: OpenAI Codex (GPT-6.2 Astra) <noreply@openai.com>
